# Configuration values allowed to sit with nothing saying what they are for.
#
# Written like scripts/check-telemetry.allow, whose header argues the shape and
# why a line lacking its reason is ignored rather than honoured: the name, a
# colon, then the justification.
#
# Expect this file to stay short. A value that genuinely needs no prose is
# rarer than it feels while writing one -- `port` looks obvious until somebody
# asks which side of the container it is on.
#
# A compose value that is right to be a literal, rather than something .env
# can reach, is `compose.yaml:NAME=literal: why`. The `=literal` is what tells
# the two excuses apart: a value may have prose and still be a knob nobody can
# turn, which is the fault check-config.py's header names third.

compose.yaml:DJANGO_EXTRA_ALLOWED_HOSTS=literal: a fixed prefix for the names the probe dials, with the interpolation .env reaches after it; the comment above it argues it.
compose.yaml:BACKEND_ORIGIN=literal: compose-network wiring, the backend by service name; the comment above it says why it is resolved rather than written.
compose.yaml:UPSTREAM_ORIGIN=literal: the same wiring, the frontend by service name, for the TLS proxy.
compose.yaml:GF_AUTH_ANONYMOUS_ENABLED=literal: a decision about the telemetry profile, not a knob; the comment above it says why.
compose.yaml:GF_AUTH_ANONYMOUS_ORG_ROLE=literal: the same decision.
compose.yaml:GF_AUTH_DISABLE_LOGIN_FORM=literal: the same decision.
